**Sort state is global to the whole table.** `arrow` has no notion of *which* column is sorted, so the icon shows on every sortable header and the cycle in `nextArrow` remembers state across columns. Suggest:

```ts
protected readonly sort = signal<{ field: string | null; direction: SortArrow }>(
  { field: null, direction: 'none' },
);

protected arrowFor(title: string): SortArrow {
  const { field, direction } = this.sort();
  return field === title.toLowerCase() ? direction : 'none';
}

protected onHeaderClick(title: string) {
  if (!this.isSortable(title)) return;
  const field = title.toLowerCase();
  const current = this.sort();
  const direction = current.field === field
    ? this.nextArrow(current.direction)
    : 'arrow_downward';
  this.sort.set({ field, direction });
  this.sortChange.emit({ field, direction });
}
```

Then render `arrowFor(title)` in the template instead of the bare `arrow()`.

When the user changes a row's item from a numeric field to a text field (or vice versa), `values()[index]` keeps the old typed value — e.g. pick `Calories`, type `5`, switch to `Mfr`: the stored value is still the number `5` while the input is now `type="text"`. Worth normalizing here:

```ts
onItemChange(index: number, value: string) {
  this.items.update((arr) => {
    const next = [...arr];
    next[index] = value;
    return next;
  });
  // reset the value so the type matches the new item
  this.values.update((arr) => {
    const next = [...arr];
    next[index] = TEXT_FILTER_ITEMS.includes(value) ? '' : 0;
    return next;
  });
}
```

`CEREAL_API_HOST` duplicates the `http://localhost:4300` literal that already exists inside `cereal-service.ts` (`BASE_URL`). The two will drift the moment you point at staging or prod. Suggest moving the host into `src/environments/environment.ts` and deriving both this constant and `BASE_URL` from it:

```ts
// environment.ts
export const environment = {
  apiHost: 'http://localhost:4300',
};
```
```ts
// cereal-display.constants.ts
import { environment } from '../../environments/environment';
export const CEREAL_API_HOST = environment.apiHost;
```
```ts
// cereal-service.ts
const BASE_URL = `${environment.apiHost}/api`;
```

Subscription is never torn down. Service-scoped `BehaviorSubject` so it won't leak the value, but the callback keeps firing after the component is destroyed. Cheap fix:

```ts
import { takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
import { DestroyRef } from '@angular/core';

private readonly destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);

ngOnInit(): void {
  this.cerealService.isLoggedIn
    .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef))
    .subscribe((value) => this.isLoggedIn.set(value));
}
```

(Or, longer-term, expose `isLoggedIn` from the service as a `Signal<boolean>` via `toSignal()` and drop the subscription entirely.)
